Title: The Concern's Claim
Order: 2
Production Code: 1x02

EXT. HALYARD STATION - DOCKING RING - DAY

AMB: a station over capacity, too many languages at once

Boxed text on arrival:

"Halyard was built for a few dozen ships a week. There are more than that in the docking queue alone, and the station's doing its best not to look nervous about it."

A Tessera Concern courier finds the party before they've cleared customs.

INT. HALYARD STATION - CONCERN OFFICE - CONTINUOUS

AMB: recycled air, a data terminal humming

[[Osei Marchant]] doesn't stand when they enter. [[Auger]] does — warm, immediate, already halfway to a joke.

AUGER
You're the ones who beat the whole Verge to the Loom. I told the Director someone would. I didn't think it'd be a crew this size.

OSEI MARCHANT
(pleasant, unhurried)
Tessera holds the only filed license to salvage that structure. That's not a threat, Captain — it's a fact you're welcome to verify.

CASS IBARRA
We found something out there before your license existed.

OSEI MARCHANT
Then I'd very much like to buy it from you. Generously.

GM note: this is the scene to play Auger as genuinely likable — competent, funny, useful. Nothing here should read as villainous. The relation marking Auger's true nature (world/auger.md, production_code 1x09) is not revealed at this point in the material, and this scene shouldn't accidentally telegraph it.

AUGER
(lower, just to the party, once Marchant's attention is elsewhere)
Off the record — don't sell it to her outright. Let her pay you to keep looking. Better deal for everyone still standing at the end of this.

It's good advice. That should feel a little strange, later, once the table knows why Auger gave it.

Marchant offers a scouting contract: quiet passes along the Loom's outer hull, first look at anything found in exchange for payment and Concern protection at the site.

OSEI MARCHANT
Halyard's about to get a great deal more crowded, Captain. You'll want friends with resources before that happens.

MUSIC: STING - a decision point, no resolution yet

GM note — contingencies: if the party refuses the contract or is openly hostile to Marchant, the Concern doesn't become an enemy outright — she simply withdraws the offer and tightens access, which should read in `1x05`'s tracked_fields as a cooler Concern posture than if they'd accepted. Auger's advice and warmth should land the same regardless of how the party feels about Marchant; Auger isn't lying about anything tactical here, only about what they are.
